  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Train Length, Pole Crossings, and Speed Units",
    "description": "Build the core train model: crossing a point means the whole train moves one train-length, with quick km/hr ↔ m/s conversion.",
    "teachingApproach": "Open with the front-to-rear crossing picture, then build unit conversion, pole-time, hidden length, hidden speed, same-speed scaling, pillar counting, and a platform bridge. Each worked event is followed immediately by a fresh parallel question.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 20, Problems on Trains. Approved scope: Teach that a train has cleared a pole, signal, tree, or standing person only when its rear has passed the point, so the distance travelled equals the train's own length. Cover km/hr to m/s using ×5/18 and m/s to km/hr using ×18/5, then use distance = speed × time to find crossing time, train length, or speed. Include same-speed length scaling, minute/second conversion, and the chapter's equally spaced pillar-count idea where the starting pillar makes the count one more than the number of gaps. Keep the mental model visual: front enters, rear clears, one train-length has moved."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's make train crossings feel like one moving picture.\n\nThe key question is always: how far must the front of the train move before the rear has completely cleared the object? Start with the simplest object of all — a point.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A train crossing a pole is a tiny event with one important detail: the front passes first, but the event is not over until the rear also clears the pole.\n\nSo a 180 m train must move 180 m to completely pass a pole.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "That gives the first train rule:\n\npoint object → distance = train length\n\nIf the train moves at 54 km/hr, convert first: 54 × 5/18 = 15 m/s.\n\nThen time = 180 ÷ 15 = 12 s.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
